<BaseLayout
  title="MCU & Scalar Cores"
  description="Architectural deconstruction, Level 1 CoreMark &amp; Dhrystone empirical benchmarking, cross-core taxonomy, and native silicon dossiers across evaluated open-source RISC-V scalar processors."
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <div class="page-header accent-yellow">
    <div class="badge badge-accent">OPEN-SOURCE SCALAR ARCHITECTURES &bull; HARDWARE SPECTRUM &amp; BENCHMARKS</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">Understanding Microcontroller &amp; Scalar Cores</h1>
    
    <!-- First-Principles Intuitive Introduction -->
    <div class="intro-narrative">
      <p class="intro-p intro-lead-highlight">
        Behind almost every electronic device you interact with daily—from the microwave on your kitchen counter and the anti-lock brake sensor in your car to smart thermostats, IoT sensor beacons, and flash memory controllers—sits a tiny embedded microcontroller core.
      </p>
      <p class="intro-p">
        Unlike the high-power, multi-gigahertz processors in laptops and smartphones that burn tens of watts running full Linux operating systems, deeply embedded scalar cores operate silently in the background. They sip single-digit milliwatts (or even microwatts) of battery energy, occupy tiny fractions of a square millimeter of silicon die, and execute real-time firmware loops with cycle-by-cycle deterministic predictability.
      </p>
      <p class="intro-p">
        In hardware engineering, however, <strong>silicon area directly equals manufacturing cost</strong>. If you give a core a wide 32-bit parallel adder, 32 registers, dynamic branch predictors, and a 5-stage pipeline, it completes instructions rapidly (high instructions-per-cycle, or IPC), but takes up substantial silicon area. If you strip it down to a minimalist state machine or a 1-bit serial shifter, the core shrinks to near-zero area—but an instruction might require dozens of clock cycles to finish.
      </p>
      <p class="intro-p">
        This page is a top-down educational guide and empirical benchmark report covering <strong>open-source RISC-V scalar architectures</strong> across the hardware spectrum. Below, you will explore their internal datapath schematics, step through their clock-by-clock execution in an interactive simulator, compare their empirical CoreMark and Dhrystone scores measured on cycle-accurate HDL simulators (Verilator, CXXRTL, Icarus, and GHDL), and discover which core best fits your silicon or FPGA design constraints.
      </p>
    </div>

