        <!-- 2.1.3 DARKRISCV DEEP-DIVE -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <span class="pill-badge badge-yellow">CORE 3</span>
            <h4>DarkRISCV: Asynchronous LUT-RAM Distributed Architecture (~8.2 kGE)</h4>
          </div>
          <p>
            Marcelo Samsoniuk's <strong>DarkRISCV</strong> leverages an FPGA-specific architectural primitive: <strong>distributed LUT-RAM</strong>. Unlike standard synchronous block memories (BRAM) which mandate an unavoidable 1-cycle read latency, LUT-RAM supports purely combinational, asynchronous reads:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Combinational Decode &amp; Fetch:</strong> Instruction memory and data registers respond combinationally to address inputs within the same clock period. This collapses instruction fetch and decode into a single phase.</li>
            <li><strong>Near Single-Cycle Execution:</strong> Because memory wait-states are eliminated when running out of LUT-RAM, DarkRISCV achieves an empirical <strong>CPI of 1.64</strong>, delivering <strong>0.6435 CoreMark/MHz</strong> in just ~8.2 kGE of logic.</li>
            <li><strong>The Silicon Trade-off:</strong> This design is tailored for FPGAs (Xilinx Artix-7/Spartan-6, Lattice iCE40). In standard ASIC cell libraries, asynchronous RAM arrays require latch-based memories which are sensitive to clock skew and timing hazards.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

