  <!-- SECTION 3: ARCHITECTURAL SIMILARITIES -->
  <section id="architectural-similarities" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-accent">&sect; 3. COMMON ARCHITECTURAL DNA</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Similarities: The Shared RISC-V Foundation</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        Despite massive differences in pipeline depths and gate counts, all evaluated scalar cores share an identical theoretical and practical foundation. This architectural convergence is what guarantees software portability across open silicon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="similarities-grid">
      <!-- Similarity 1 -->
      <div class="sim-card">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num">01</span>
          <h4>Base ISA Compliance (RV32I / RV32E)</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          Every single core implements the unprivileged RISC-V base integer instruction set specification. All cores support:
        </p>
        <ul class="sim-list">
          <li><strong>Fixed 32-bit Instruction Length:</strong> Standardized 6 instruction formats (R-type, I-type, S-type, B-type, U-type, J-type) with uniform opcode placement in bits [6:0] to simplify instruction decode logic.</li>
          <li><strong>Standard Register File:</strong> 32 general-purpose 32-bit registers (<code>x0&ndash;x31</code>) with register <code>x0</code> permanently hardwired to constant zero, or 16 registers (<code>x0&ndash;x15</code>) under the RV32E embedded profile (DarkRISCV, SERV-E).</li>
          <li><strong>Two's Complement Arithmetic:</strong> Identical sign-extension rules, arithmetic right shifts (<code>sra</code>), and unsigned comparison logic (<code>sltu</code>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 2 -->
      <div class="sim-card">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num">02</span>
          <h4>Machine-Mode (M-Mode) Privileged Control &amp; CSRs</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          All evaluated cores operate natively in Machine Mode (M-mode), the mandatory hardware root privilege tier:
        </p>
        <ul class="sim-list">
          <li><strong>Standard Trap Handling Registers:</strong> <code>mstatus</code> (interrupt enable bits), <code>mtvec</code> (trap vector base address), <code>mepc</code> (exception return PC), <code>mcause</code> (trap etiology identifier), and <code>mtval</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Performance Instrumentation Counters:</strong> 64-bit hardware cycle counter (<code>mcycle</code>) and retired instruction counter (<code>minstret</code>), read via standard <code>rdcycle</code> and <code>rdinstret</code> pseudoinstructions.</li>
          <li><strong>Deterministic Exception Vectoring:</strong> Support for direct trap vectoring or vectored mode for timer, software, and external interrupts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 3 -->
      <div class="sim-card">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num">03</span>
          <h4>Unified Toolchain &amp; Software ABI Portability</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          A single C firmware binary compiled with the open standard GNU toolchain runs on any of the evaluated cores:
        </p>
        <ul class="sim-list">
          <li><strong>Calling Convention (ILP32 / ILP32E):</strong> Function arguments passed in <code>a0&ndash;a7</code>, return values in <code>a0/a1</code>, saved registers in <code>s0&ndash;s11</code>, and stack pointer <code>sp</code> kept 16-byte aligned.</li>
          <li><strong>Standard Startup Routine (<code>crt0.S</code>):</strong> Reset vector execution initializes stack pointer (<code>sp</code>), clears <code>.bss</code> section, populates <code>mtvec</code>, and branches to <code>main()</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Shared Compiler Backend:</strong> All cores consume code produced by upstream <code>riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc</code> without vendor-specific proprietary toolchain forks.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 4 -->
      <div class="sim-card">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num">04</span>
          <h4>Deterministic SCORE Benchmarking Verification</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          All benchmark results reported in SCORE share an uncompromising verification methodology:
        </p>
        <ul class="sim-list">
          <li><strong>Identical Workload Sources:</strong> Standard CoreMark v1.0 and Dhrystone v2.1 benchmark source code compiled with matching optimization flags (<code>-O3 -funroll-loops</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Zero Mocked Data:</strong> Every cycle, instruction, and execution metric is extracted from cycle-accurate HDL simulations (Verilator, Icarus, CXXRTL, GHDL).</li>
          <li><strong>Automatic CRC Checksum Validation:</strong> CoreMark matrices, linked lists, and state machines are validated against expected reference CRC checksums before benchmark acceptance.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: MICROARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCES -->
  <section id="microarchitectural-differences" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-coral">&sect; 4. MICROARCHITECTURAL DIVERSITY</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Differences: Engineering Trade-Offs</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        While ISA compliance guarantees software compatibility, the physical hardware implementations differ wildly across pipeline depths, multiplier hardware, branch prediction, and memory interconnects.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Differences Dimension Cards -->
    <div class="diff-cards-deck">
      <div class="diff-spec-card">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag">PIPELINE HAZARDS</span>
          <h4>Pipeline Depth vs. Branch Penalty</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          Deeping the pipeline increases maximum clock frequency (<em>F</em><sub>max</sub>) but increases the latency penalty incurred on branch mispredictions and load-use data hazards:
        </p>
        <ul class="concept-list">
          <li><strong>1-Stage Serial (SERV):</strong> Zero pipeline hazards. Operands shift serially; branch instructions take 32 clock cycles with zero speculative stalls.</li>
          <li><strong>2-Stage In-Order (Ibex, SCR1 MIN, NEORV32):</strong> Fetch and Decode/Execute stages. Branch penalty is exactly <strong>1 clock cycle</strong> on taken branches.</li>
          <li><strong>3-Stage Harvard (Hazard3, RV12):</strong> Fetch, Decode, Execute/Writeback. Employs single-cycle branch hazard forwarding to keep branch penalties to 1–2 cycles.</li>
          <li><strong>4-Stage Superscalar (VeeR EL2):</strong> 2-wide dispatch with 4 execution pipes. Branch misprediction costs <strong>3 clock cycles</strong>, necessitating a dynamic Gshare branch predictor.</li>
          <li><strong>5-Stage Classic (VexRiscv GenFull):</strong> Classical IF-ID-EX-MEM-WB with full data forwarding bypass network and load-delay slot resolution.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="diff-spec-card">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag">ARITHMETIC UNITS</span>
          <h4>Multiplier &amp; Divider Microarchitectures</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          The implementation of the M-extension (`mul`, `mulh`, `div`, `rem`) represents the single largest area-versus-performance trade-off in embedded processors:
        </p>
        <ul class="concept-list">
          <li><strong>Software Emulation (SERV, RV32I-only):</strong> Multiplications execute via software library functions (<code>__mulsi3</code>), consuming 80 to 250 clock cycles per operation.</li>
          <li><strong>Bit-Serial / Multi-Cycle Shift-and-Add (Ibex small, PicoRV32):</strong> Shifts operands over 32–34 clock cycles, saving silicon area by avoiding wide DSP multipliers.</li>
          <li><strong>Fast Radix-4 Non-Restoring Divider (Hazard3, SCR1):</strong> Resolves 2 bits of quotient per clock cycle (16 cycles for 32-bit division).</li>
          <li><strong>Pipelined Single-Cycle DSP Multiplier (VeeR EL2, Ibex maxperf, VexRiscv):</strong> Dedicated 32&times;32 multiplier completing in 1 cycle, crucial for CoreMark matrix multiplications.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="diff-spec-card">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag">MEMORY FABRIC</span>
          <h4>Memory Buses &amp; Bus Handshakes</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          Interconnect architecture dictates how efficiently the processor can access instructions and data without inducing pipeline wait-states:
        </p>
        <ul class="concept-list">
          <li><strong>Open Bus Interface (OBI) (Ibex):</strong> Advanced pipelined request/grant protocol with independent address and data phases.</li>
          <li><strong>Wishbone b4 Pipelined (SERV, NEORV32, VexRiscv):</strong> Classic open standard bus interface enabling master-slave crossbar interconnects.</li>
          <li><strong>Dual AHB-Lite / AHB5 (Hazard3, SCR1, VeeR EL2):</strong> Industrial standard offering separate instruction and data channels with burst pipelining.</li>
          <li><strong>AXI4 / AXI4-Lite (VeeR EL2, VexRiscv, PicoRV32 AXI):</strong> Full crossbar connectivity with separate read/write data channels for high-bandwidth SoC integration.</li>
          <li><strong>Direct LUT-RAM (DarkRISCV):</strong> Asynchronous memory interface mapped directly to dual-port distributed FPGA RAM.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="diff-spec-card">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag">SECURITY HARDENING</span>
          <h4>Silicon Security &amp; Fault Injection Resistance</h4>
        </div>
        <p>
          For cryptographic enclaves and safety-critical automotive deployments, raw CoreMark scores are secondary to physical attack resistance:
        </p>
        <ul class="concept-list">
          <li><strong>Dual-Core Lockstep (DCLS) (Ibex in OpenTitan):</strong> Runs two identical cores with staggered clock offsets to detect physical laser/clock fault injection attacks.</li>
          <li><strong>Physical Memory Protection (PMP):</strong> Hardware access control isolating secure enclave memory regions from untrusted software (up to 16 regions in Ibex and SCR1).</li>
          <li><strong>Dummy Instruction Insertion:</strong> Randomizes pipeline execution timing to defeat Differential Power Analysis (DPA) side-channel attacks.</li>
          <li><strong>Register File Parity &amp; ECC:</strong> Detects and corrects bit-flips in register files and tightly-coupled memories (ICCM/DCCM in VeeR EL2).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

